WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.610
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.760
In this video, we talk about
nuclear binding energies.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.960
But before we get
started on this topic,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.170
I would like you to have a look
at this table or this diagram,

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.610
which shows nuclear abundances
in our solar system,

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.640
so how many atoms
of the various types

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.670
are present in our solar system.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.720
And you see this super
interesting structure.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.650
Most of it is hydrogen here.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.130
And then there seems to be
some sort of an excess of iron.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:42.580
And, you know, this table goes
all the way to lead and beyond.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.880
There's a little
bit of a gap here.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.370
So how is this possible?

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.000
How did this particle get
there in the first place?

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.585
Why is there some which are
more frequent than others?

00:00:55.585 --> 00:00:57.020
A very interesting
question which

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.380
we will be able to answer
at the end of the discussion

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.850
of nuclear physics.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.490
And the first starting
point is to understand

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.210
why nuclei are stable in the
first place, what holds them

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:07.870
together.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.640
And that's the discussion
of binding energies.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.140
So we can very simply write
the binding energy down.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.860
We just sum up all the
ingredients, the number

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:26.360
of protons and electrons, times
Z, the number of neutrons, A,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.020
the mass number
minus Z. And then

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.620
we subtract from this the
mass of the nuclei itself.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.100
What remains is
the binding energy.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.990
Just for record, the mass of the
proton, the mass of the proton,

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.240
the mass of the neutron,
you see that the neutron

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.820
is slightly heavier
than the proton,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.270
and the neutron itself
decays into the proton.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.370
A free proton does not decay.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.330
However, inside the nucleus,
a proton can also decay.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.340
And then we have the
mass of the electron.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.530
You see there's a
factor of almost 2,000

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.240
between the mass
scales of an electron

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.460
and the mass of a neutron.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.780
For all practical purposes,
we can ignore this,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.780
but if it comes to
precision measurements,

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.680
then the mass of the neutron
which is 1/2 MeV becomes--

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.670
of an electron, which is
1/2 MeV, becomes relevant.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.450
This plot here shows the
average binding energy

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.170
per nuclei as a function
of the mass number.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.000
And you see that
with the exception

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.720
of those light elements,
you see that this

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.450
is fairly stable and in
the range of 7.5 to 9 MeV.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.230
You also see that there seem
to be a maximum around iron,

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:44.150
which then leads to an
advantage in gaining energy

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.650
when you are--

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.080
gaining energy going
to lower-energy state

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.290
when you go in this direction
and in this direction.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.140
This part is called fission,
this part is called fusion.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.010
Both processes, because we go to
a more energy-preferred state,

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:02.862
are possible.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.420
They can be used in
order to attain energy

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.750
from nuclear processes.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:17.420
This diagram here
can be parameterized.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.090
And the rest of
this video, we'll

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:24.260
talk about a very
popular parameterization

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.880
of the binding energy.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.610
This is semi-empirical.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.560
It's called the
Weizsager formula,

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.040
because it was proposed by
a German called Weizsager.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.010
Sometimes it's called the
semi-empirical mass formula,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.200
and sometimes the
discussion is summarized

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.210
in the liquid-drop model.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.820
And you'll see why in a second.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.160
What you see here is very
similar to where before we

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.920
can calculate the mass and
from that the binding energy

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.870
by having those
first elements here.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.080
And this part then here
is our binding energy.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.460
And there's 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 terms,
which we're discussing now

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.410
on the next slide.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.710
What is shown here
is a parameterization

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.840
so you can fit the
data and get a best

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:15.080
estimate for the individual
parameters in this equation.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.010
All right.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.200
So as the name said,
liquid-drop model,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:27.200
we can think about, in some
essence, about a nuclide atom

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.160
as being built out of a soup, a
liquid of protons and neutrons

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.080
which are bound together.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.660
So the first term
which contributes

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.920
to the binding energy is
the so-called volume term.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.500
This dominates the
binding energy.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.490
And it's proportional
to the number--

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.980
the mass number.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:50.450
Remember, the mass number is
proportional to the third power

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.330
of the radius,
hence proportional

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.410
to the volume of the nuclei.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:02.960
And you know, this contributes
with about 16 MeV per nucleon,

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.860
per proton and neutron.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.540
And from this, you can
conclude that the nuclear force

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.290
must be very short range.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:12.460
Why is that?

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.080
Because in order for
the binding energy

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.450
to depend dominantly
on the volume,

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:23.740
the individual nuclei can
only see its nearest neighbor.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.870
So this corresponds to a short
range force which is roughly

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.240
of the distance of two nuclei.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.740
If any given nuclei would be
able to see everybody else,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:43.970
we would see a term quadratic in
the number of nuclei available.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.910
As a result of this, you can
calculate the central density,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:54.470
which is about 0.17 nucleons
per cubic femtometers

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:59.230
or an average distance between
protons and neutrons of 1.8

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.480
femtometers.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:01.980
OK.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.750
So they are really
tightly packed.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.560
The size of a proton
is about a femtometer.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.550
All right.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.170
However, the
protons and neutrons

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.730
which are on the surface
of this construct, they

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.700
see less nuclei--

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.000
they see less nuclei,
nucleons around him.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.120
So therefore, the binding
energy needs to be reduced.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.590
And it needs to be
reduced with the area

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:36.000
of the surface of the nucleus.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.570
So they need to be proportional
to r squared, and therefore

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:45.700
proportional to A, the
mass number, to the 2/3.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:46.660
OK.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.370
Then the protons in the nucleus,
they are electrically charged.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.470
So they are going to get apart.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:56.110
And that itself also
reduces the binding energy.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.070
So this is proportional to
the number of charges squared.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.310
And then you normalize
this by the radius.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:04.480
So charge squared over--

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:05.710
no, normalized.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.590
Charge squared over r.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:13.070
So this is q squared
over r, Coulomb term.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:13.730
All right.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:17.430
There's two more terms,
which are quite interesting.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.840
The first one is
sensitive to the asymmetry

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.680
between the number of neutrons
and the number of protons.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.950
And it can be explained by
the Pauli exclusion principle,

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:31.940
which allows only two
identical fermions, neutrons,

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.070
or two protons to occupy
the same energy state.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.580
So you basically fill
up the energy states.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.460
Now, as is shown in
this picture here,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.670
you reach the
lowest energy state

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.640
if the number of neutrons
and the number of protons

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.050
is actually the same.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.660
You have higher energies
if there is an asymmetry

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:52.300
between those two numbers.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.110
But we have yet
another term which

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:57.980
is sensitive to the asymmetry
between the number of neutrons

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:02.750
and protons, which reduces
the binding energy.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.630
And last but not least,
the pairing term,

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.220
which has a very similar origin.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.460
So what we are looking
at here is the energy

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:15.530
is lower if you have an even
number of neutrons or protons.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:18.560
It's higher when we
have an odd number.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.080
So you can have an odd
number for the protons

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.960
or the neutrons, in
which case A is odd.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:28.040
And the worst case, or
the worst energy state,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:33.000
is achieved having both the
protons and the neutrons odd.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.510
So that's why this is a little
bit more complicated way

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:36.500
to write this.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:39.679
You have those three
different cases--

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:43.850
number of protons and
number of neutrons even,

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:49.470
A odd, or both Z and N odd,
in which case A is even--

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.230
just to add to the
confusion a little bit.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:52.730
All right.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:55.380
Then you can make a drawing
of the binding energy

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.440
as a function of the mass.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.560
And you see, again, those
individual terms, the volume

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.200
energy, the volume
term here constant,

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.260
the volume plus surface
reduced, the volume plus

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.870
surface Coulomb further
reduced, and then

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:12.030
all terms put together here.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.930
And you see again this binding
energy parameterization,

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.540
which we just discussed--
we saw the actual values.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:24.950
There's a maximum
around here for iron.

